sting effect in  canadian politics  well after donald trump leaves office  donna  okay mercedes stephenson in ottawa  thanks silently spreading the  coronavirus coming up new insight into  how often people with no  symptoms are infecting others  students in southern ontario will have  to wait until january 25th before  returning to in-person learning  ontario's premier says one in five  children under the age of 13 who have  been tested  are carrying covid19 and as a result  schools will stay closed for now  but in alberta a decision has been made  to send all students back to class on  monday  as heather urix west explains the covet  19 situation across the country is far  different now than at the start of the  school year  like so many parents alicia higa is just  trying to take things  day by day between broken  links that don't work and them getting  frustrated me getting frustrated  it's i'm at a point where it's just  you do what you can and just move on two  of her three kids are learning virtually  right now  sitting at a computer for hours on  monday they'll head back to class  the decision to resume in class learning  on january 11th is based on  carefully considering the importance of  attending school in person  as well as the latest evidence of cases  dropping  in all school-related age groups in  december  the bremer says he's confident kids can  return to classes  safely but this expert isn't so sure  i do think that there's plenty of reason  to be concerned and i'm certainly  concerned about  the idea of having children return to  the classroom  as early as next week dr jim kilner says  it's still  not clear that infection levels in  alberta are actually on the decline  and at current levels of community  spread he says there is growing evidence  that open schools  can lead to more transmission among  asymptomatic kids  the thing that's evolved as well is an  understanding that schools can be  contributing  to the rise of disease in the community  i don't think anybody can comfortably  save that schools  don't contribute to transmission of  infection  it's why in southern ontario where daily  case numbers are still on the rise  schools will remain closed to in-person  learning for at least  another two weeks i know this is a tough  one  it's tough for everybody and we know  those parents have worked hard at this  and having the virtual learning is not  easy trying to manage  their two different schedules my own  schedule  and my four-year-old i can't imagine if  there are people working from home right  now  back in alberta higa says she's not  worried about covet 19.  her kids have never had a case in their  classes but the reality  is it's january and the pandemic is now  in a very different place  than it was in the fall heather urex  west global news calgary  there's new data from the u.s showing  how asymptomatic and pre-symptomatic  people  are spreading the coronavirus modeling  done by the u.s centers for disease  control and prevention shows  asymptomatic people transmit more than  half of all coven 19 cases  35 percent are people who infect others  before they show symptoms  24 percent are from people who never  develop symptoms  researchers say the findings reinforce  the importance of following  all public health guidelines whether you  feel sick  or not bad influencers still ahead a  canadian public health official takes an  all expense paid trip  to jamaica  a senior manager at the public health  agency of canada has apologized for  taking an all expense paid trip to  jamaica  ignoring all the warnings about avoiding  non-essential travel dominique baker  flew with a friend to jamaica in  mid-november and stayed  in an upscale resort the trip was paid  for by air canada vacations which  she promotes as a social media  influencer  the timing just wasn't right and i  shouldn't have gone  and i urge anyone thinking of getting  away this winter  to wait until this pandemic is over  good advice she failed to take baker is  the manager of public health canada's  office of border and travel health  which is focused on keeping communicable  diseases out  of canada next how fragile is democracy  even in a rich and powerful country  what happened yesterday in washington  d.c will be the legacy of donald trump's  presidency  mob's running riot through the u.s  capitol summoned there by trump himself  stoking their anger telling them the  vice president had the power to overturn  the election result  the last desperate reckless act by a man  who came close to melting the  foundations  of the american republic  this is how democracy can crumble driven  by a leader who refused to accept he  lost an election  trump supporters did this in his name  intoxicated by four years of his  incendiary rhetoric  believing his baseless claims the  election was stolen  people motivated to action by lies  they stormed the u.s capitol carrying  confederate flags  vandalizing intent on stopping the  peaceful transfer of power  because their leader told them to we  will never give up we will never concede  it doesn't happen  you don't concede when there's theft  involved  our country has had enough we will not  take it anymore let's have trial by  combat  that rallying cry answered by a mob  even erecting a makeshift gallows a  noose just steps from the u.s capitol  this was an attempted coup and i wish we  could say we couldn't see it coming  but that isn't true we could  see it coming the past four years we've  had a president  who's made his contempt for our  democracy  our constitution the rule of law  clear in everything he has done  a vivid chilling reminder democracy is  fragile  and america is not exceptional the  question remains whether the instigator  of this  donald trump will ever be held  accountable  and that is global national for this  thursday i'm donna friesen  in the heart of washington the u.s  capitol is being fortified the high  fencing to remain in place  for at least 30 days thanks for watching  hope to see you here again  tomorrow take care of yourselves bye-bye
 

